WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.760
Malcolm Grant: We move on now to items relating
to patient insight, which I will invite Tim

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.010
Kelsey to introduce.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:12.880
Just before I do I should just record that
members of the Board yesterday participated

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.300
in a dementia session.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:22.300
I thought very well hosted for us by the Alzheimer's
Society, to try to develop within us a sensitivity

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.650
and an understanding of the challenges faced
by people suffering from those diseases that

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.780
make up dementia, including Alzheimer's disease.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:37.969
The Board, I think, is very keen to get under
the skin of a number of these issues that

00:00:37.969 --> 00:00:44.129
patients face in dealing with the NHS, and
more generally in society with conditions

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:49.109
such as dementia, which are not visible often
to outsider observers.

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:55.511
We are also, of course, more generally across
the parallel field of mental health, subject

00:00:55.511 --> 00:01:00.159
to an obligation to give parity of esteem
to mental health and to try to understand

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:07.270
how we can bring the NHS closer to meeting
some of the quite exceptional challenges in

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.270
that area.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.700
For us, patient insight is absolutely critical
to what we do.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:13.700
Tim, over to you.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:14.730
Patient Insight
Tim Kelsey

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.870
National Director for Patients and Information
Prioritising patients in every decision we

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:17.870
make
Thank you, Chairman.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:23.460
As we said in previous meetings NHS England
is fundamentally and centrally committed to

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.810
prioritising patients in every decision it
makes, and the commitment we made some time

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.100
ago was to be very transparent in the way
we both listen to patients and the broader

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:37.510
public, and then demonstrate our response
to issues that are raised.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:44.960
In a sense, the point of this initiative is
to create a web-based tool, an app which will

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.380
enable us to monitor on a daily, weekly, monthly
basis, the kind of conversations that are

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:56.210
being had about the NHS just amongst people
in our communities, and more specifically

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:01.490
the conversations that are being had about
particular services by individual patients

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:07.640
as they receive them and subsequently, is
a really important resource for the NHS England.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:12.300
It is also really important that we are very
transparent about the way we interact with

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.300
that data.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:19.299
Broadly put, I think this is about us beginning
a new and, in some part, real time conversation

00:02:19.299 --> 00:02:21.909
with communities that we are directly serving.

00:02:21.909 --> 00:02:29.280
Sources of data
Before I quickly show you some of the actual

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.530
pages from the new tool, it is worth just
reflecting on the sources for this data.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:39.090
This is a very new kind of thing for the NHS;
we do not have ready made, real time sources

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:43.269
of data on what people are telling us about
services so we have tried to be as creative

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:45.299
as we can as we move towards that objective.

00:02:45.299 --> 00:02:51.810
You will see the priority placed on things
like whether patients are actively likely

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.370
to recommend local services to friends and
family members.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:58.360
That is a new data source which actually has
started coming live in April; you will that

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.190
is already in the tool.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:04.090
With staff we are equally interested in understanding
where the staff would recommend the services

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.510
that they are working on, to their friends
and family, and that data at the moment is,

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:08.510
for example, annual.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:13.840
We are about to initiate a monthly data collection
which will give people a chance -- not just

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.799
people who work in NHS, but much more broadly
people that work in the health and care services,

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:23.029
to tell us whether or not they feel that their
service is worthy of their recommendation

00:03:23.029 --> 00:03:24.469
or not.

00:03:24.469 --> 00:03:30.410
We have drawn on real time data sources like
the kinds of feedback that hundreds and thousands

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:36.249
of people are giving to NHS Choices, the public
information service that we now are commissioned,

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:40.969
on behalf of the whole system, and other similar
data.

00:03:40.969 --> 00:03:44.370
I think people will find interesting the way
in which we have worked with partners to try

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.819
and harvest real insight from social media.

00:03:48.819 --> 00:03:54.209
There is a whole world of commentary that
is being given to us on Twitter and on Facebook,

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:58.609
which at the moment we are not properly listening
to, just to set it in context.

00:03:58.609 --> 00:04:02.579
In that context too I would really like to
thank my team, the team who has worked on

00:04:02.579 --> 00:04:07.069
this, and with colleagues across the board,
it has been a phenomenal piece of work that

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:10.530
really has taken the midnight oil, but at
least we are at the beginning now.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.669
Rollout
In terms of rollout the idea is that this

00:04:13.669 --> 00:04:15.659
service, which is currently live, we test
now.

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:22.130
We need to seek feedback from the community,
within NHS England and beyond, over the next

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:25.590
couple of months, and then it will go fully
live to the general public in August, where

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.060
again we will be looking for feedback.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.060
It is a journey.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.670
It will not be perfect at the start but at
least it is a beginning.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.730
Insights
If I can quickly just show you what it looks

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.570
like.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:44.130
At the moment we have broken it into two parts
and here we have the app-based presentation

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.610
of it.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:46.610
We have called it Insights.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:50.360
This is the national level so you can see
there are various lenses through which we

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.120
are listening to people.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:57.460
The first of those is in the top left: would
patients recommend services, friends and family?

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.980
As I mentioned, that data is actually from
the Midlands and East where they have been

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:05.740
running a pilot on that for some time now.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:08.250
Underneath that we have an analysis of complaints
data.

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.640
There is an enormous richness in the complaints
data the NHS receives.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:17.880
It has not been well unbundled so there are
some insights we are receiving there about

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.760
different kinds of complaints, about different
sorts of services.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.960
Top right is what is known as a wordle.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:29.890
This is a means of visualising what people
are telling us from that real time data that

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:32.870
I was mentioning before, the sort of feedback
we are getting on NHS Choices.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:39.900
These are the words that are being emphasised
in comments that are being made right at this

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.030
minute.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:48.000
Then we have the staff data underneath that:
do staff recommend the hospital services?

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.670
This visualisation of the data will change
on a daily, weekly and monthly basis, but

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:58.020
largely it will be completely refreshed every
week, so the idea is to keep it live and interesting

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.340
so that we do continue to pay attention to
it.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:04.610
Sourcing of the data
The sourcing of the data: an important factor

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:11.770
is obviously articulated within the tool behind
those little information icons.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.990
An important point here is related to the
staff survey data.

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:16.680
At the moment, as I mentioned, we have annual
data.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.940
We now have arrangements to get that to monthly
quickly.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:23.150
I think this will be a very important source
of data, not just for us but for all our colleagues

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:26.090
who work in health and care.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:28.830
Then we have a different lens.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:32.150
That was Pete really looking at what patients
are telling us about services, how they are

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:33.950
complaining, so this is a different lens.

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:38.470
This is the lens of the conversation that
is being had about health and care, without

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:39.470
health and care.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:43.460
So this is just what are people talking about
in their daily lives, what are they complaining

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:45.590
about and what issues are they raising?

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:50.780
Again, you can see that at the moment those
words -- cold, pain -- are coming out strongly

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:56.000
from the very broad family of data that is
being analysed.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.650
We have broken out a few of the key tweets
that are being most highly re-tweeted, and

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:04.240
obviously this changes in real time so this
is just a snapshot.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:11.530
We can see underneath that -- I think we have
a slide on this; we have a daily perception.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:17.430
On the bottom left we have created a new measure,
a new index of public perception of the NHS

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:23.460
and that is based on a daily analysis of 18
different topics that are analysed across

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:28.270
all social media and aggregated into an index
score which we can then plot on a graph.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:32.230
You will be able to break that down into the
individual kinds of things that are being

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:33.700
spoken about.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:37.810
None of this is definitive nor statistical
but I think it provides us with a very important

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:44.970
basis for understanding really what is going
on in the communities we are serving.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:51.870
Summary
To finish, this is the timetable, testing

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:56.430
out there with the public from August in a
beta version but people will be able to see

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:00.390
it, play with it, give us commentary back
and then we go live formally, as it were,

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:05.561
in November, and that then will I hope become
an instrument of formal Board reporting to

00:08:05.561 --> 00:08:07.580
this public meeting from that point onwards.

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.420
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Tim.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:12.590
Speaker: Sorry, can I just state that it is
quite difficult to see from the back here,

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:16.260
Tim, so we can get some copies made for the
members of the public if they would like them?

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.180
Tim Kelsey: Sorry, this is on the public website;
this presentation is available on the public

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:19.180
website.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:20.500
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:21.500
Victor?

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:29.460
Victor Adebowale: I think this is really good,
really strong and in the sense that, for a

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:37.740
while, there has been a lack of qualitative
data that we can use to measure so well done,

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.401
I think this is a really good first step.

00:08:39.401 --> 00:08:48.120
I just want to make sure though that we are
able to use this information in a number of

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.510
ways, and one of the ways I am particularly
interested in is: so we have all this information,

00:08:53.510 --> 00:09:00.820
is part of this work about telling us about
the experiences of the people at the sharp

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:07.690
end of the inverse care law because if we
want to drive that inequalative gap we want

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:16.040
to get rid of it, then the experience of the
people that are challenged are very important.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:22.790
I am just interested in what your views are
on how we use this fantastic step forward

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:26.430
to actually drive into those communities,
those individuals?

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:32.610
Tim Kelsey: That is an incredibly important
question.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:37.020
I think the Board is aware that we have just
launched a partnership with UK Online to take

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:42.580
100,000 of the most disadvantaged people into
basic online health literacy.

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:47.331
There is absolutely a question about how we
make sure that this listening that this is

00:09:47.331 --> 00:09:53.820
something which is totally inclusive and so
we are really listening and understanding

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:58.090
communities who are not naturally users of
the internet or the web or digital tools.

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:03.640
I am rather hoping that the kind of conversation
we can now have as we move towards the November

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:08.550
formal launch with organisations like yours
and Ciarán's and the voluntary sector as

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:14.850
a whole will help us develop the information
so we are targeting it much more effectively,

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:19.340
or more effectively at those communities that
we need to listen to the hardest.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:24.250
Victor Adebowale: Very quickly - are we also
going to use other methodologies that bring

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:29.720
views into this that do not rely on the use
of internet, because I am aware that there

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:31.950
are eight million people who do not access
this stuff.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.970
I just want to make...

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:38.961
Tim Kelsey: So just to make the point - this
is, of course, accessed over the web; really

00:10:38.961 --> 00:10:41.950
this is about mobile phones.

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:47.720
Almost all this data is either reviewable
on a mobile phone, hence the iPad app, or

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:51.990
it is in fact contributable on a mobile phone.

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:55.750
If you take, for example, homeless populations
-- we know that homeless populations are far

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:59.840
greater users of mobile telephones than you
would expect.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:04.710
They are not pay-as-you-go but they are receiving
information over mobiles, that is how people

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:06.220
get hold of them.

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:11.850
We need to understand the particular characteristics
of different communities that we want to listen

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:14.170
to and understand how we can take this to
them.

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:20.850
Broadly, put there is no new platform more
inclusive than the mobile or the smart phone,

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:24.680
so think of this less as a web tool and more
as a mobile application.

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:27.700
It is a very important point that you are
raising.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:28.700
Malcolm Grant: Bill?

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:29.700
Thanks, Tim.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:34.550
Bill McCarthy: Just to reinforce Victor's
point really -- one of the commitments we

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:41.430
made in the business plan is, wherever we
can in presenting, collecting and publishing

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:50.060
data and information, we will do it with an
understanding of different geographical areas,

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:54.899
but also people with different characteristics
so that we can understand at quite a deep

00:11:54.899 --> 00:12:02.760
level which parts of this country are getting
left out, whether it is geography or whether

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:03.920
it is groups of people.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:08.910
This is a very early stage where Tim's team
and my team are working quite closely to make

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:12.899
sure that we can make these data sources as
rich as possible so we can do the right thing

00:12:12.899 --> 00:12:15.670
for people who are at risk of getting left
out.

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:17.080
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:18.080
Moira?

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:19.190
Moira Gibb: Thank you, Malcolm.

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:20.610
Two questions for Tim.

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:23.240
One, you said contributable, does that mean
people can risk...

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:24.240
Tim Kelsey: Can make comments.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:25.240
Moira Gibb: You can; yes.

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:26.240
Okay, thank you.

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:30.149
That is really helpful because it triggers
you to say something, does it not, when you

00:12:30.149 --> 00:12:31.149
learn something.

00:12:31.149 --> 00:12:37.310
My second one, as you said this is not statistical
but of course it is numbers, but what people

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:42.800
will really be interested in are trends, and
given the speed of updating information how

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:50.600
will you capture trends, matter on a yearly
basis or on a daily basis depending on what

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:51.660
the information is?

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:55.280
What I am thinking about is the information
that disappears, where will it go as it were,

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:58.120
so that people can look back to longer trends?

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:00.140
Tim Kelsey: It will not disappear.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:01.660
We are starting this now.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:06.640
Some of these tools are new so they do not
have a history to them; others are not new

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:11.720
and we can already track the trends in things
like staff surveys and so on.

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:17.530
The idea is absolutely that this will become
an ongoing, analytic machine which will offer

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:22.120
a number of different sorts of functionalities
-- obviously trend analysis being amongst

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:26.780
the most important -- so that is absolutely
the plan.

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:31.340
In a sense, I have just given you a very quick
overview, and I did not even mention, which

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:34.580
I should have done of course, is that there
is the England level but you will also be

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:39.760
able to drill into the hospital trust level
too.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:44.040
As time goes by we will be able to offer much
more longitudinal kind of insights, which

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:45.470
are the ones that matter.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:47.000
Malcolm Grant: Naguib?

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:52.920
Naguib Kheraj: Two points on the sourcing
of the data.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:59.880
Building on Victor's point really about how
do you know you are getting the data from

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:02.330
a broad section of people?

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:06.960
I can see how you get the social media stuff
without having to go out anywhere because

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:13.870
you can just capture that, but how do you
select who contributes public opinion?

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:21.150
The second question was really about the staff
and patient input because NHS England does

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:29.990
not operate any services, so the vast majority
of staff and all the patient input is accessible

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:33.440
only through the people who run those services.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:41.490
Do we have some sort of hard agreement with
the operators of services that say they are

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:44.840
going to provide this information to us on
a certain periodicity?

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:45.840
Is that how it works?

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.580
Tim Kelsey: That is how it works.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:53.200
We have contractual arrangements through the
standard contract for hospitals, for example,

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:57.120
to provide the friends and family test on
a specified basis.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:03.070
Also, we legally have the responsibility of
setting the standards for data collection

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:07.730
in services that we commission, so we have
a number of levers that we can use to support

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:13.120
and help providers develop these data services.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:14.899
We do have those.

00:15:14.899 --> 00:15:22.160
Bruce Keogh: A follow on, Tim, from Naguib
-- you have addressed the issue of accessing

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:27.500
opinion -- is there some way of refining it
so that we can also access solutions, because

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:34.400
we have 60 million people out there who have
a hugely vested interest in the NHS, and that

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:37.951
is where the intellectual capital for solving
many of our problems will lie.

00:15:37.951 --> 00:15:42.089
Tim Kelsey: I think that is a really great
point.

00:15:42.089 --> 00:15:45.570
This in a way, is a breakthrough, it is kind
of sterile as well.

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:47.700
It will tell us something but it is not getting
to the point.

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:53.580
We have this enormous asset which is our staff,
our citizens; we have not really properly

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:59.269
unlocked yet in terms of reshaping, redesigning
the way we approach healthcare in so many

00:15:59.269 --> 00:16:00.269
different ways.

00:16:00.269 --> 00:16:04.470
I am hoping -- it is an important challenge
for all of us -- that we can move quickly

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:08.970
from merely a reporting tool to something
that is much more fundamentally about a network

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:14.250
of engaged citizens and patients who are absolutely
helping us design the future of health and

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:15.250
care.

00:16:15.250 --> 00:16:23.420
That sounds a very lofty objective but it
is absolutely where we should be heading to.

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:37.370
Margaret Casely-Hayford: In 
that case, Tim, is there going to be a way

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:38.600
of mending expectations?

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:39.600
If you have uploading of information on a
daily basis, changing of analysis on a daily

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:40.600
basis, there will be an expectation that there
will be responses almost as quickly, and to

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:50.250
an extent that puts an enormous amount of
pressure on the executive and everyone else

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:51.250
within the organisation.

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:56.180
Clearly, we will be responding, obviously
on even a monthly basis because that does

00:16:56.180 --> 00:17:04.510
not give the opportunity for proper analysis
and qualitative thought to deal with the information.

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:09.520
Tim Kelsey: Just something very quickly, I
think there are different moments in the cycle

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:12.430
of response.

00:17:12.430 --> 00:17:19.650
Bruce intervened in Leeds, a few weeks ago,
very rapidly because of a very serious issue

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:23.880
in relation to potential patient care which
may have emerged, say, from data that is collected

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:24.880
here.

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:28.390
That is one response which absolutely people
should hold us to account for doing very instantly,

00:17:28.390 --> 00:17:32.309
but then there are other types of responses
which, of course, will need more thought.

00:17:32.309 --> 00:17:38.480
I think that is something, in a way, that
the data will help us resolve but I do agree.

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:43.049
This is a very common kind of resource in
other sectors; I am sure John Lewis would

00:17:43.049 --> 00:17:48.210
have their fingertips all over social media,
and I think that the customer in those contexts

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:52.790
is pretty sanguine about the way in which
an organisation needs time to respond.

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:56.300
I think, in a sense, if we can develop a kind
of maturity about the conversations we are

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:59.590
having with the people in the communities
we are serving, I think they will respond

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:01.080
with equal maturity.

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:05.640
I do agree that we will be exposing here things
which may need very urgent response and we

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:09.440
will have to just accept that as additional
pressure.

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:11.280
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Tim.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:16.900
I think this is a general sense of excitement
within the Board about this as being a major

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:20.100
step through to do something which has not
been done before.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:25.940
I think the range of data that can be released
and the trends are going to be critically

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:29.170
important for the shared journey because it
is not simply the Board that is dictating

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:33.712
the future of the NHS; it is a shared operation
and listening to the patient voice is going

00:18:33.712 --> 00:18:36.450
to be a critically important part of it.

00:18:36.450 --> 00:18:42.140
I would also commend the point that Bruce
made: for its future development it is a platform

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:45.170
for solutions as well as for diffusion of
information.

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:50.000
I think there needs to be a cut-off point
in terms of the data that can go in there

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:55.050
because we do not want to overflow too much
data; we want to identify high points, high

00:18:55.050 --> 00:19:00.740
trends and then invite engagement from those
who use it.

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:06.610
Also, I thought that the point that Victor
made about how we reach the audiences that

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:14.120
are not otherwise having access to the internet
-- the issue about mobile telephony is also

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:15.270
a critically important one.

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:20.900
The adaptability of the dashboard to all modes
of transmission I think is critical.

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:25.750
Thank you for that and I think the Board warmly
endorse it.

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:30.090
Right, may we move to your next paper, Tim,
on the patient focus?

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:31.260
Patient Focus
Tim Kelsey

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:33.890
National Director for Patients and Information
Vision and purpose

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:39.920
Since NHS England was established as the commissioning
board authority there has been a proper focus

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:45.330
on how best we can express our values and
vision, particularly for our own staff.

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:51.840
This has been, as I think everyone has said,
a very turbulent transition and one of the

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:56.730
things that is very important is that people
have a very clear sense of their own mission

00:19:56.730 --> 00:20:00.970
and how they contribute to the core purpose
of the health service.

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:06.299
So we, over the last few months, have worked
with around 500 members of our team, so that

00:20:06.299 --> 00:20:11.330
is getting close to not quite everyone but
a large number of the team who have given

00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:16.220
up their time in the midst of all other businesses
to focus on this core question for them, which

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:21.660
is what do they see as the core mission and
vision of NHS England?

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:26.530
Some people will think, 'Well, surely you
already know what your core vision of the

00:20:26.530 --> 00:20:30.049
NHS is before we start' but actually this
is a very, very important process for us to

00:20:30.049 --> 00:20:31.669
have gone through with our staff.

00:20:31.669 --> 00:20:34.639
What we are presenting here today is just,
in a way, the beginning of a journey.

00:20:34.639 --> 00:20:39.429
This is an interesting, dynamic process in
which, as people come and join in NHS England

00:20:39.429 --> 00:20:48.880
they will all participate in the refinement
and development of these core visions and

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:49.880
values.

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:55.790
The single idea
In a sense, the distillation of all those

00:20:55.790 --> 00:21:01.620
workshops and discussions with staff, and
with many others too, has been to come to

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:04.650
a single idea from which everything flows.

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:09.450
That idea is that we are here to deliver high
quality care for all, for now and for future

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:10.910
generations.

00:21:10.910 --> 00:21:15.720
The importance of that statement is that we
are commissioning services in health and care,

00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:18.040
not just for today but for tomorrow.

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:32.419
From that very simple but very rich statement
of mission, comes a whole range of insights

00:21:32.419 --> 00:21:36.730
with which we can then shape the way we individually,
as members of the team but more broadly as

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:39.050
a team, go about our business.

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:45.450
If I can, assuming people have the paper,
take you to page four of the presentation.

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:49.680
This presentation is, in a sense, an exercise
which has been distributed to all staff; it

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:52.190
is a core part of the induction process.

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:56.169
We are getting very good feedback in that
it is beginning to help people understand

00:21:56.169 --> 00:21:57.730
really what it is they are here to achieve.

00:21:57.730 --> 00:22:04.150
It is on page four if you are able to locate
it.

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:10.660
There is hopefully a relative, useful, helpful
diagram which I hope takes that single idea

00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:16.480
and expresses how it becomes a set of tools,
a set of behaviours, which individual members

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:18.970
of the team can start deploying in their own
work.

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:22.900
You will see that in this context, if the
idea is high quality care for all, and in

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:26.549
that context our job is to commission it,
then what we are really doing is our purpose

00:22:26.549 --> 00:22:31.200
is to create the culture and conditions for
service, for staff on the frontline to do

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:32.520
their jobs properly.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:38.660
I think the purpose statement captures very
richly -- again, drawn from the observations

00:22:38.660 --> 00:22:40.820
of various staff what it is that we are here
to do.

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:45.010
And that then feeds into a series of values
and behaviours which -- it is just worth reflecting

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:46.010
on.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:49.220
There are six of them and I honestly think
that people on our team, us ourselves, should

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.800
be very much holding ourselves to account
for delivering them, so we must prioritise

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:54.640
patients in every decision we take.

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:56.309
We must listen and learn.

00:22:56.309 --> 00:22:58.500
We must be evidence-based.

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:03.610
We must be open and transparent, inclusive
and constantly striving for improvement.

00:23:03.610 --> 00:23:08.360
These seem obvious things but take the many
things we could have prioritised, and delivering

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:11.200
us with six I think is a really helpful and
important framework.

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:17.540
It was interesting also that listening to
people as they started to express their own

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:24.730
personal reasons for having joined this enterprise,
having committed themselves to this enterprise,

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:27.220
some of the things that people are coming
up with is the core reason for that.

00:23:27.220 --> 00:23:32.740
It was that they regarded NHS England as the
guardian of the NHS -- they regard their role

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:39.410
as, frankly, a privilege; not just important,
vital, but as a privilege and something sacrosanct.

00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:42.970
I think that was an important message that
came across.

00:23:42.970 --> 00:23:47.150
People are very committed to the idea that
we, in everything, do need to be compassionate.

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:50.480
Jane has, of course, led on that in relation
to nursing but right across the piece -- that

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:55.210
is the value which people want this organisation
to be promoting.

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:59.320
And equally, the inequalities issue which
I just raised: the idea that we should be

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:05.140
an engine of social justice is fundamental
to the reasons for many people joining the

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:06.140
organisation.

00:24:06.140 --> 00:24:10.060
And again, I hope it is captured in some of
the language that we are now proposing is

00:24:10.060 --> 00:24:16.230
the language that becomes, as it were, the
lexicon for how we express ourselves moving

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:17.230
forward.

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.980
Business plan metrics
Just one other final point to make is that

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:25.110
from those behaviours and the values, so from
the idea cascading downward to the behaviours

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:30.680
therefore that that implies for staff, we
then also of course have the specific metrics

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:37.280
against which the organisation as a whole
will be measured, and that leads us into page

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:44.100
21 of the presentation which reminds us of
the 11 business plan metrics.

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:48.200
For those in the live stream who have not
had a chance to read our business plan I would

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:52.030
strongly recommend it; it is an unusually
brilliant document for a public service organisation

00:24:52.030 --> 00:24:53.100
to have produced.

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:58.010
One of the reasons for its brilliance is the
fact that it is offering us up against a series

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:00.820
of very values-based, outcomes-based measured.

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:07.100
NHS England will only be successful if patients
are satisfied with the services they are receiving

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:08.210
in their local communities.

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:13.080
That is an amazingly brave and brilliant standard
against which we should be judging ourselves,

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:17.960
and that flows very directly from the core
idea that this is about high quality care

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:18.960
for all.

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:20.145
I am very happy to take some questions on
that.

00:25:20.145 --> 00:25:22.850
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Tim, for that presentation.

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:23.990
Victor?

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:31.789
Victor Adebowale: Again, I think it is genuinely
brave and if we get this right it will move

00:25:31.789 --> 00:25:34.970
the needle on the quality of services to the
public generally.

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:41.250
I believe that these things are generally
not just about the aspiration but they are

00:25:41.250 --> 00:26:01.410
about the way in which 
we change the ongoing conversation.

00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:06.210
What your thoughts are on how we maintain
the conditions for that test so that it does

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:11.880
not become a static piece of work that is
out there that creates a huge expectation

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:17.559
and energy, and then in the white heat of
delivery becomes less and less relevant when

00:26:17.559 --> 00:26:21.600
people are facing their day to day pressures.

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:28.789
I think, for me, the test is what happens
when the plan hits pay dirt, so just some

00:26:28.789 --> 00:26:33.330
thoughts on how we do that.

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:43.590
Maybe there are not any thoughts; it is just
a view I guess; it is about what happens in

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:44.590
reality.

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:47.760
Tim Kelsey: Just the act of the Board saying,
'These things really matter.

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:54.990
In fact, these things are the most important
things for us', is a pretty important step

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:59.240
in the direction of ensuring that this is
a dynamic, living process.

00:26:59.240 --> 00:27:03.170
The other thing, which is in the paper and
I did not mention, is the incredibly good

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:17.750
work that has been done by the team who work
for me in patients' information, from a recent

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:19.330
meeting, but this is fundamental to that.

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:24.320
What we anticipate and want to encourage is
the idea that people joining the organisation,

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:29.230
people refreshing themselves with the values
of the organisation will themselves be redesigning

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:31.789
and refining this as we go forwards.

00:27:31.789 --> 00:27:37.000
I think we should expect to be coming back
here in six months, a year with a description

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:42.070
of how we have refined, how we have changed
it in the light of how the organisation has

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:45.559
developed and what people are telling us as
they come into it.

00:27:45.559 --> 00:27:46.870
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:47.870
Ciarán?

00:27:47.870 --> 00:27:50.600
Ciarán Devane: I think this is hugely important.

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:59.049
I have been involved by courtesy of Tim dragging
me into it and I think the process by which

00:27:59.049 --> 00:28:03.340
we got to this was really impressive; the
engagement from staff, the creativity, the

00:28:03.340 --> 00:28:05.919
thinking.

00:28:05.919 --> 00:28:08.672
I think there are two things going on: one
was people were learning what NHS England

00:28:08.672 --> 00:28:14.600
was about and that was everybody who was involved,
but the second thing was it was trying to

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:19.661
gain a consensus, the people coming from all
these different points of view, about their

00:28:19.661 --> 00:28:25.289
experience of the NHS system was where they
were coming from and having to bin that was

00:28:25.289 --> 00:28:28.070
at least as important in the process.

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:31.700
Working that through into an induction business
plan, I think, was a great job.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:35.940
I think us getting behind this a board and
saying, 'This is how we are going to hold

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:43.679
ourselves and everybody in NHS England to
account' is really, really important.

00:28:43.679 --> 00:28:48.990
The last one is I was trying very hard to
say how I could change it, improve it, criticise

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:57.510
it, etc, and the best I could do was try and
sneak one word in on page 22, which is the

00:28:57.510 --> 00:28:58.780
one around accountability.

00:28:58.780 --> 00:29:03.880
If we were able to say, 'We are in para-clinical,
professional and patient leadership at all

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:07.990
levels of the NHS' then I think it reinforces
some of the stuff we are about.

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:12.039
With that said, I think it was a great piece
of work and everything we do and talk about

00:29:12.039 --> 00:29:13.860
we should be referring back to this.

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:18.520
It is about high quality care for all now
and in the future; that is why we exist.

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:19.980
It is a good bit of work.

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:20.980
Malcolm Grant: Ros?

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:25.450
Rosamond Roughton: I just want to say I think
this is really important as well for our relationships

00:29:25.450 --> 00:29:26.450
with CCGs.

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:31.070
I think, as we have been building the new
system we have been really clear that our

00:29:31.070 --> 00:29:36.860
ability to deliver better outcomes will depend
on how we work with CCGs.

00:29:36.860 --> 00:29:41.640
Whilst we have been working with staff we
have doing similar exercises with CCGs, and

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:48.429
the feedback from CCGs is very congruent with
this, so a shared purpose around patients,

00:29:48.429 --> 00:29:50.400
listening and learning, evidence based.

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:57.450
I think in terms of holding ourselves to account,
getting feedback through NHS clinical commissioners

00:29:57.450 --> 00:30:03.580
about how CCGs perceive us, and the way that
we behave will be a really good test of whether

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:07.800
the values set out here, that we are living
them in all our actions.

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:13.360
As you were saying, Victor, when it gets difficult,
are we holding true to these behaviours?

00:30:13.360 --> 00:30:15.400
Malcolm Grant: Ed?

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:18.120
Ed Smith: Again, positive.

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:23.450
One of the things that interests me is the
extent to which we use storytelling of what

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:29.230
is actually happening out there and bring
that into play in terms of using those stories

00:30:29.230 --> 00:30:31.370
as an improvement mechanism.

00:30:31.370 --> 00:30:36.030
I think there is a very strong link here to
the leadership academy and the extent to which

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:43.770
we use this at every level of development
in the leadership academy, alongside live

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:47.460
storytelling of what is actually happening.

00:30:47.460 --> 00:30:57.870
Moira Gibb: Malcolm, could I just ask whether
CSUs were also involved in part of NHS England

00:30:57.870 --> 00:30:59.050
for the time being?

00:30:59.050 --> 00:31:01.130
Tim Kelsey: I would have to check.

00:31:01.130 --> 00:31:02.350
I should know the answer to that question.

00:31:02.350 --> 00:31:08.210
Moira Gibb: Could I just follow on, Malcolm,
and ask: care services are included throughout

00:31:08.210 --> 00:31:09.210
this?

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:10.210
Tim Kelsey: Yes.

00:31:10.210 --> 00:31:16.500
Moira Gibb: And of course we are not the commissioning
body for social care services so there is

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:18.250
a complexity in that?

00:31:18.250 --> 00:31:21.370
Tim Kelsey: There is, but I think the really
important thing to capture, or we are all

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:25.270
going to go slowly mad as we try and link
our direct function to the job we are trying

00:31:25.270 --> 00:31:28.730
to achieve, is that this is about creating
the culture and conditions for health and

00:31:28.730 --> 00:31:29.730
care services.

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:34.159
It is not necessarily that we can only do
that for services we directly commission;

00:31:34.159 --> 00:31:39.060
we can in fact set the tone and work in partnership
with other commissioners to do that so I think

00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:40.060
it is quite important.

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:43.929
That is what that purpose box is trying to
say.

00:31:43.929 --> 00:31:50.880
Margaret Casely-Hayford: This is controlling
from Barbara's point really -- to that extent,

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:59.900
where we are in relationship with partners
we need to foster champions who will promote

00:31:59.900 --> 00:32:05.240
this story, and say we have our own leadership
programme but the promoting the champions

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:10.820
within other entities would actually strengthen
the message and I think that helps to deliver

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:14.550
some of what Victor was asking -- how do we
move it forward on the ground?

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:17.190
Malcolm Grant: Yes, absolutely.

00:32:17.190 --> 00:32:18.190
David?

00:32:18.190 --> 00:32:23.750
David Nicholson: A couple of things about
change management really in all this.

00:32:23.750 --> 00:32:29.640
One of them for me is that very often this
kind of work and this kind of paper is not

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:31.210
completely well received in the NHS.

00:32:31.210 --> 00:32:36.890
It is kind of seen as something over here
that we will do when we have time to do it,

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:40.919
where the important job is to get on with
- but I do think that.

00:32:40.919 --> 00:32:44.460
I certainly never believed that; I do not
think it is right.

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:49.200
If anyone ever did not believe it you would
have to look at the Francis Report and the

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:54.799
importance of being really clear about the
culture you are trying to create and then

00:32:54.799 --> 00:32:57.330
prosecuting that culture at every opportunity.

00:32:57.330 --> 00:33:03.380
It genuinely saves lives; getting the right
culture; that is the first thing.

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:11.870
The second thing, I think, is that sometimes
in my experience of supervisory or commissioning

00:33:11.870 --> 00:33:19.140
bodies is that they spend quite a lot of time
telling everybody else how they should value

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:23.029
patients and get them at the centre, and all
the rest of it, but actually do not do it

00:33:23.029 --> 00:33:26.779
themselves because somehow they feel that
that is their job; just to tell everybody

00:33:26.779 --> 00:33:27.779
else to do it.

00:33:27.779 --> 00:33:32.600
What we know is that, in any change management
thing, you start with yourself and you start

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:35.480
with your own people and you start with your
own organisation.

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:41.070
That is why this is so, so important to us,
and that is why the organisation and development

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:46.710
plan, which underpins this is just as important,
is a mechanism for making that happen.

00:33:46.710 --> 00:33:54.620
I think the great news is that people are
really up for this but people are sceptical

00:33:54.620 --> 00:33:59.980
about whether actually, when we hit the road
or whatever way we describe it, whether it

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:05.650
will get lost, so it is particularly important
I think for the Board to publically recognise

00:34:05.650 --> 00:34:13.820
and support this and say it to our members
to make sure that we prosecute it really carefully

00:34:13.820 --> 00:34:15.929
over the next period.

00:34:15.929 --> 00:34:22.740
Malcolm Grant: Absolutely, and change starts
here, I think, around the Board table but

00:34:22.740 --> 00:34:24.230
it is not imposed.

00:34:24.230 --> 00:34:28.010
What I find very attractive about this model
is the extent that it has built up through

00:34:28.010 --> 00:34:30.159
the conversation with 500.

00:34:30.159 --> 00:34:37.830
I am still just astonished that last month
we had 70-odd staff and this month we have

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:39.510
7,000.

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:44.090
If I had joined the staff I would be just
completely bewildered actually of what this

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:48.530
was were it not for an exercise such as this
that develops a common sense of purpose.

00:34:48.530 --> 00:34:54.669
It is not the Chairman or even the Chief Executive
defining what that is; it has been a genuine

00:34:54.669 --> 00:34:56.480
exercise in engagement.

00:34:56.480 --> 00:35:04.290
I think that we need to continue to work very
closely as members of the Board with staff,

00:35:04.290 --> 00:35:10.270
to try to ensure that what we achieve with
our own staff also, through their behaviour,

00:35:10.270 --> 00:35:13.870
has a knock on impact in the relationships
that they have with the rest of the system.

00:35:13.870 --> 00:35:19.740
I am very encouraged by what Ros says about
CCGs, for example, and the intimacy of our

00:35:19.740 --> 00:35:26.369
relationship with them and the extent to which
we are in support of a facilitative role I

00:35:26.369 --> 00:35:29.970
hope has a very considerable knock on effect.

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:34.980
I think to signify the importance of the exercise
and our commitment to it we should put a definite

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:39.900
date in the diary in six months time to come
back to measure it, to understand how it is

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:44.012
working and to review it and to see what further
steps may be appropriate then.

00:35:44.012 --> 00:35:47.380
May we bring it back to the Board in six months?

00:35:47.380 --> 00:35:50.920
Naquib Kheraj: I think then, Chairman, it
would be a good idea to get some playback

00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:53.910
from staff of how much they have actually
absorbed it.

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:58.890
It is one thing to take people through this
material; it is another thing to see how much

00:35:58.890 --> 00:36:00.480
of it they have retained.

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:04.830
Jo-Ann Wass: We can maybe pick some of that
up through the staff barometer; I will pick

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:05.980
that up as an action.

00:36:05.980 --> 00:36:06.980
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:36:06.980 --> 00:36:11.390
Moira Gibb: There is also a challenge to us
about whether we have lifted as well -- and

00:36:11.390 --> 00:36:16.140
that has to start, as David says, with our
behaviour.

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:22.980
Malcolm Grant: Subject to the Ciarán Devane
amendment on page 22 may I invite you to approve

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:26.540
and enthusiastically endorse the paper?

00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:27.950
Thank you very much.

